Here are the core takeaways, senior-level interview questions, and practical coding translations for Autowiring Collaborators and Method Injection.

📝 Main Points to Note Down

• Autowiring Collaborators: Allows the IoC container to implicitly resolve and inject bean dependencies without explicit <property/> or <constructor-arg/> declarations. [1] (https://www.scribd.com/document/917645264/Autowiring-Concept-in-Spring)
• The Legacy Autowiring Modes:
	• no: Default mode. Dependencies must be defined explicitly using references or target class annotations.
	• byName: Inspects property bean names and automatically links them with a bean containing the exact matching ID/name.
	• byType: Matches properties by their exact class or interface type. It fails instantly with a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException if more than one matching bean type exists.
	• constructor: Identical to byType but applies strictly to constructor arguments. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-autowire.html), [2] (https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/spring-autowiring/), [3] (https://www.scribd.com/document/917645264/Autowiring-Concept-in-Spring), [4] (https://www.codebegun.com/learn/spring-boot/core/autowired-annotation)
• Excluding Beans from Autowiring: You can isolate a bean from type-based autowiring loops by configuring autowire-candidate="false" (or autowireCandidate = false inside @Bean). This blocks the bean from satisfying standard annotation-driven @Autowired points. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-autowire.html)
• Method Injection (The Lifecycle Mismatch Problem): Occurs when a long-lived singleton bean needs to work with a short-lived prototype bean. Because the singleton is instantiated only once, standard dependency injection injects the prototype exactly once at startup, completely defeating the prototype's multi-instance purpose. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-method-injection.html), [2] (https://dzone.com/articles/method-injection-spring), [3] (https://blog.frankel.ch/method-injection-with-spring/)
• Lookup Method Injection: The container solves lifecycle mismatches by overriding a method on the singleton bean dynamically at runtime using CGLIB bytecode generation. Each time that method is invoked, it dynamically fetches a brand-new instance of the prototype bean from the ApplicationContext. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-method-injection.html), [2] (https://dzone.com/articles/method-injection-spring)

🧠 Senior-Level Questions & Answers


Q: How does Spring dynamically implement @Lookup (Lookup Method Injection) under the hood? What are the strict limitations placed on the target class architecture?

• Answer: Spring utilizes CGLIB (Code Generation Library) to intercept lookup method invocations. At application startup, the container generates a dynamic subclass overriding the method annotated with @Lookup (or defined via <lookup-method>). It replaces your stub implementation with a bytecode routine that programmatically executes beanFactory.getBean(TargetClass.class) behind the scenes. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-method-injection.html)
• Because CGLIB relies on runtime subclassing, neither the enclosing singleton class nor the lookup method itself can be marked as final. Additionally, lookup method injection is structurally incompatible with beans generated via Java configuration @Bean factory methods because the container does not control class creation in the same way as classpath scanning. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-method-injection.html)

Q: What is the architectural purpose of setting autowire-candidate="false" on a bean definition, and how does it affect name-based vs. type-based resolution?

• Answer: Marking a bean with autowire-candidate="false" removes it from the pool of eligible candidates for type-based autowiring (such as standard @Autowired or byType processing). This is heavily utilized when you have multiple beans of the same type but want to pin down a fallback or prevent an alternative implementation from causing structural collisions. [1] (https://smartprogramming.in/tutorials/spring-framework/autowiring-in-spring), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-autowire.html)
• However, this attribute only isolates the bean from type-matching criteria. If another component deliberately requests the bean via an explicit name match (such as utilizing @Qualifier("exactBeanName") or via byName autowiring), Spring will still successfully inject the collaborator because name resolution bypasses autowire-candidate checking. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-autowire.html)

Q: If you cannot use @Lookup due to classes being final or due to working within a pure @Bean Java Configuration, how do you cleanly bypass the singleton-prototype lifecycle mismatch?

• Answer: If CGLIB bytecode generation is impossible or blocked, a senior engineer should use functional wrappers such as ObjectFactory<T>, Provider<T> (JSR-330), or Spring's TargetSource proxying mechanisms.
• By injecting an ObjectFactory<MyPrototypeBean> into the singleton instead of the raw bean instance, you don't break encapsulation or tie your business code to the low-level ApplicationContext interface. The singleton simply calls objectFactory.getObject() exactly when needed, which safely pulls a completely fresh prototype instance from the container on demand. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/annotation-config/autowired.html), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-method-injection.html)

💻 Practical Coding Translation: XML to Modern Java Config


Legacy XML Lifecycle Mismatch Fix:

xml
<beans>
    <!-- Short-lived prototype bean -->
    <bean id="commandNotification" class="com.example.CommandNotification" scope="prototype"/>

    <!-- Long-lived singleton capturing a fresh prototype via method injection -->
    <bean id="commandProcessor" class="com.example.CommandProcessor" scope="singleton">
        <lookup-method name="createNotification" bean="commandNotification"/>
    </bean>
</beans>
Use code with caution.

Modern Java Configuration Equivalent:

In modern Spring Boot architectures, when classes are defined explicitly or via scanning, we avoid abstract class overrides by using type-safe functional containers like ObjectFactory:
java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.ConfigurableBeanFactory;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Configuration
public class MethodInjectionAlternativeConfig {

    // 1. Prototype Bean Definition
    @Bean
    @Scope(ConfigurableBeanFactory.SCOPE_PROTOTYPE)
    public CommandNotification commandNotification() {
        return new CommandNotification();
    }

    // 2. Singleton Bean Definition consuming ObjectFactory
    @Bean
    public CommandProcessor commandProcessor(ObjectFactory<CommandNotification> notificationFactory) {
        return new CommandProcessor(notificationFactory);
    }
}

// --- Implementation Classes ---

class CommandNotification {
    public void dispatch(String message) {
        System.out.println("Dispatched from fresh instance: " + this.hashCode() + " | " + message);
    }
}

class CommandProcessor {
    // The factory acts as a type-safe provider to bridge the lifecycle gap cleanly
    private final ObjectFactory<CommandNotification> notificationFactory;

    public CommandProcessor(ObjectFactory<CommandNotification> notificationFactory) {
        this.notificationFactory = notificationFactory;
    }

    public void processTask(String taskName) {
        // Every invocation safely retrieves a completely unique prototype reference!
        CommandNotification freshNotification = notificationFactory.getObject();
        freshNotification.dispatch("Processing: " + taskName);
    }
}
